# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw sensor data — far too large for git (a single .mcap here is 23 GB).
# Keep these on local disk or object storage, not in the repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
recordings/
*.mcap
*.pcap
*.bag

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datasets: track only the manifests that describe each dataset, never the
# extracted point clouds. The manifest is enough to rebuild a dataset from
# its source recording, and it is the thing that says which generation
# profile built it.
#
# Datasets are nested one folder per profile now (datasets/full-sweep/<run>/),
# so these have to match at any depth — the old fixed-depth rules stopped
# matching the moment the layout gained a level.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/datasets/**
!/datasets/**/
!/datasets/**/manifest.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Training artifacts — the big regenerable stuff. This is ~6 GB and it grew
# the repo's history to 3.2 GB before it was caught.
#
#   caches/       pooled point-cloud cache, one folder per profile (~1.9 GB)
#   experiments/  every trained fold: best.pt, last.pt, predictions (~3.9 GB)
#
# Two things ARE kept, on purpose:
#   exported/     the deployable models — the point of the repo
#   reports/      the conclusions (summary.md/json plus figures, ~7 MB). Small,
#                 and it is how you know what a sweep concluded without
#                 retraining it. Add "training/reports/" below to drop these too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ignore the *contents* with /**, not the folder itself — ignoring the folder
# stops git descending into it, and then a negation below can never match.
training/caches/**
training/experiments/**
*.npy
*.npz

# ...but the exported models, the written-up results, and the README in each
# folder explaining what that folder holds are all worth keeping. Those READMEs
# are the map, and a map that only exists on one machine is no map.
!training/exported/**
!training/reports/**
!training/caches/README.md
!training/experiments/README.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local runtime output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.dashboard/
out/
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor / tooling scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/
CLAUDE.md
.vscode/
.idea/
tempCodeRunnerFile.py
.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-block scratch inside the folders un-ignored above.
#
# Git resolves each path by the LAST rule that matches it, so the
# "!training/reports/**" line above re-includes everything under that folder —
# including the __pycache__ folders, *.npz arrays and *.pyc files the general
# rules near the top of this file were written to catch. That hole is why
# git status filled up with hundreds of files that were never meant to be
# offered for commit. These rules sit at the end of the file so they win.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.npy
*.npz

# Everything a review or ablation run dumps beside its write-up: the per-run
# numbers, the rendered figures and maps, and the scratch state files it
# leaves behind. One review folder is ~145 MB across 150-odd files of this,
# against roughly two dozen in the same folder actually worth reading, and all
# of it regenerates from the scripts sitting next to it.
#
# What survives is what a person reads or runs: the *.md write-ups and the
# *.py analysis scripts, neither of which is matched here.
training/reports/**/*.csv
training/reports/**/*.png
training/reports/**/*.svg
training/reports/**/*.json

# ...except the summary file each report writes. It is the machine-readable
# half of the same conclusion the write-up states in prose, and it is how you
# know what a sweep concluded without retraining it.
!training/reports/**/*summary.json
